    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Hedgehog pathway, involved in regulating cellular growth and proliferation, has been implicated in the development of many cancers including basal cell carcinoma (BCC). BCC can be treated with vismodegib to inhibit Hedgehog signaling, but many tumors acquire resistance to this drug. The majority of BCCs are known to arise from mutations in Patched, the receptor for the Hedgehog pathway. However, a secondary driver mutation in another Hedgehog protein is generally required for oncogenesis. Given the exposure of the skin to environmental stressors, these tumors develop high mutational loads.  This high mutation rate creates a range of functional consequences whose contribution to the growing cancer remain unknown; some mutations help drive tumor development, while others cause neutral or deleterious effects. This study reveals secondary driver mutations in Suppressor of Fused (SUFU), the major negative regulator of the Hedgehog pathway, and Smoothened (SMO), the major activator of the Hedgehog pathway. We sequenced both tumor and normal skin samples from patients with early sporadic and drug-resistant BCCs. This resulted in the discovery of novel mutations in SUFU and SMO, which were functionally investigated to determine whether they help drive BCC formation and serve as drivers for vismodegib resistance development. Our results show that four of the SUFU mutations inappropriately activate the Hedgehog pathway, suggesting they act as driver mutations for BCC development. SMO variants were mostly neutral or deleterious, supporting a model in which tumors “roll the genetic dice” to generate as many mutations as possible to increase their chances of resistance development. Our results from functional characterization of these mutations further clarifies tumor progression, better specifies SUFU’s role in Hedgehog signaling, and presents novel ways in which BCCs can arise and evolve. This information is invaluable for understanding the mechanisms of tumor development in Hedgehog-driven cancers and provides new avenues for drug targets and personalized medicine.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
